    explanation: ['Carbon commonly forms four covalent bonds and can join other carbon atoms into chains or rings. A molecular formula records the number of each kind of atom, but does not show how those atoms are connected. A structural formula gives that extra information. Two compounds can therefore share a molecular formula while having different connectivity and different physical or chemical properties.', 'Hydrocarbons contain only carbon and hydrogen. Acyclic alkanes contain only single carbon-carbon bonds and follow CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. Acyclic alkenes with one double bond follow CₙH₂ₙ, but that formula alone does not prove an alkene because ring structures can share it. An alcohol contains an appropriate hydroxyl group attached to a saturated carbon. Classification needs the structure and functional group, not merely an oxygen or carbon count.'],
